Yes, I remember the general line of reasoning now. It wasn’t just about how much oil is left underground. The key point was that predictions about “running out of oil” are much more complicated than they first appear.

Here’s the version I’d save:

⸻

Will we run out of oil in the next 50 to 60 years?

The answer is probably not in the sense that people usually mean.

When people say “we have 50 years of oil left,” they are usually dividing today’s known economically recoverable reserves by today’s rate of consumption. That is a useful snapshot, but it is not a prediction of the future.

Several things change over time:

First, new oil is continually discovered. Exploration improves, and geologists identify fields that were previously unknown.

Second, advances in technology make oil that was once uneconomical practical to extract. Hydraulic fracturing, horizontal drilling, deep-water drilling, and improved recovery techniques have all greatly increased recoverable reserves.

Third, the price of oil matters. If prices rise, deposits that were previously too expensive to produce suddenly become economically worthwhile.

Fourth, demand is changing. Electric vehicles, improved fuel efficiency, renewable energy, and changes in transportation may reduce oil consumption over time, although demand is unlikely to disappear quickly because oil is also used for plastics, chemicals, asphalt, lubricants, fertilizers, and many industrial processes.

For these reasons, humanity is unlikely to experience a day when we simply “run out” of oil. Instead, production will probably become progressively more expensive, and society will gradually transition toward alternative energy sources where they are cheaper or more practical.

The bigger long-term question is therefore not “When will the last barrel of oil be pumped?” but rather “When will alternatives become more economical than extracting increasingly difficult oil?”

Historically, societies tend to transition to new energy sources before completely exhausting the old ones. Wood remained after coal became common, coal remained after oil became dominant, and oil will likely remain available long after renewable energy and other technologies have expanded.

So Vincent’s estimate of “50 or 60 years until we run out” is probably too simplistic. A more accurate statement would be that the world’s energy system will continue to evolve, with oil gradually becoming a smaller part of the mix over many decades rather than disappearing on a specific date.